Q) Explain the difference between IAM permissions and an S3 bucket policy.
IAM permissions: IAM policies define what actions an IAM user, group, or role is allowed to perform on AWS resources.
S3 bucket policy: A bucket policy is a resource-based policy attached directly to an S3 bucket. It specifies which principals can perform particular actions on the bucket or its objects.
Example:
- An IAM policy can allow a user to read objects from an S3 bucket.
- A bucket policy can grant developer-user permission to read objects from a specific bucket.
Conclusion: Both policies can control access to S3 resources. The effective permissions depend on all applicable policies and restrictions.

Q) Explain why Block Public Access is useful.
Block Public Access helps prevent accidental public exposure of S3 buckets and their objects.
Benefits:
1. Prevents unintended public access.
2. Helps protect confidential files.
3. Reduces the risk of data leaks.
4. Provides an additional layer of security.
Example: If a bucket contains personal documents, Block Public Access helps prevent anonymous users from accessing those files through public bucket policies or access control lists.
